## Defining functions

a function is a reusable block of code that runs when you call it by name
defined using def, followed by a name and parentheses
example: def greet():
print("hello") greet()
result hello
functions let you avoid repeating the same code over and over, and make programs easier to read and test in pieces

## Parameters

the named variables listed in a function's definition, act as placeholders for values that will get passed in
example: def greet(name):
print("hello " + name)
here name is the parameter — it doesn't have a real value until the function actually gets called

## Arguments

the actual values passed in when calling a function, they fill in the parameters
example: greet("Ben")
result hello Ben
"Ben" here is the argument — parameters and arguments get used interchangeably in casual conversation but technically parameters are the placeholders, arguments are the real values

## Return values

what a function hands back to whatever called it, using the return keyword
without a return statement, a function returns None by default
example: def add(a, b):
return a + b total = add(2, 3)
print(total)
result 5
a function stops running the moment it hits a return statement, anything written after it inside that function never runs
print() displays something on screen but doesn't hand a value back to be used elsewhere — return does

## Scope

scope is about where in the program a variable can actually be seen and used
a variable created inside a function only exists inside that function — trying to use it outside raises an error
example: def my_func():
x = 5
print(x) my_func()
print(x) raises NameError, x doesn't exist out here

## Local/global variables

local variable: created inside a function, only exists while that function is running, disappears once it finishes
global variable: created outside any function, at the top level of the script, accessible everywhere
example: count = 0 this is global def increment():
local_var = 1 this is local
print(count) can read the global fine increment()
a function can read a global variable by default, but trying to change it directly inside without saying so creates a new local variable instead, leaving the original global untouched. the global keyword is needed to actually modify it: def increment():
global count
count += 1

## Default arguments

lets a parameter fall back to a preset value if nothing's passed in for it when the function gets called
example: def greet(name="friend"):
print("hello " + name) greet()
result hello friend greet("Ben")
result hello Ben
default arguments always have to come after any parameters without a default

## Keyword arguments

passing arguments by naming the parameter directly, instead of relying on their position
example: def describe(name, age):
print(name, "is", age) describe(age=20, name="Ben")
result Ben is 20
since each argument is explicitly labelled, the order stops mattering, which also makes the function call easier to read at a glance

## *args

lets a function accept any number of extra positional arguments, collected into a tuple
example: def add_all(*args):
return sum(args) add_all(1, 2, 3)
result 6 add_all(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
result 15
the name args is just convention, the single asterisk in front of it is what actually matters

## **kwargs

lets a function accept any number of extra keyword arguments, collected into a dictionary
example: def show_info(**kwargs):
print(kwargs) show_info(name="Ben", age=20)
result {'name': 'Ben', 'age': 20}
kwargs is also just convention, the double asterisk is what matters
both args and kwargs are handy when you don't know in advance exactly how many arguments will get passed in

## Lambda functions

a small, unnamed function defined in a single line, using the lambda keyword instead of def
example: square = lambda x: x ** 2
square(5)
result 25
equivalent to writing: def square(x):
return x ** 2
lambdas are meant for short, throwaway functions, usually passed straight into something else rather than saved and reused

## Functions as objects

in python, functions are values just like numbers or strings — they can be stored in a variable, passed into another function, or returned from one
example: def shout(text):
return text.upper() my_function = shout
my_function("hello")
result HELLO
this is what makes higher-order functions possible, since a function can be treated as just another piece of data

## Higher-order functions

a function that either takes another function as an argument, returns a function, or both
example: def apply_twice(func, value):
return func(func(value)) def add_one(x):
return x + 1 apply_twice(add_one, 5)
result 7
map, filter, and sorted with a key argument are all built-in examples of this pattern

## Recursion

a function that calls itself, working towards a base case that stops it calling forever
example: def factorial(n):
if n == 1:
return 1
return n * factorial(n - 1) factorial(4)
result 24
every recursive function needs a base case, a condition where it stops calling itself, or it runs forever and eventually crashes with a RecursionError
anything recursion can do, a loop can usually also do — recursion just tends to fit better for problems naturally defined in terms of smaller versions of themselves, like tree structures

## Functional programming basics

a style of writing code built around functions that take input and return output without changing anything outside themselves
a pure function always gives the same output for the same input, and doesn't rely on or modify anything outside its own scope
example of a pure function versus one that isn't: def pure_add(a, b):
return a + b always predictable, no side effects total = 0
def impure_add(a):
global total
total += a changes something outside the function, less predictable
python isn't a strictly functional language like some others, but tools like map, filter, and lambda let you write in that style when it fits

## map

applies a function to every item in a sequence, returns a map object which can be turned into a list
example: nums = [1, 2, 3]
doubled = list(map(lambda x: x * 2, nums))
result [2, 4, 6]

## filter

keeps only the items from a sequence where a function returns True, drops the rest
example: nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
evens = list(filter(lambda x: x % 2 == 0, nums))
result [2, 4, 6]

## reduce

rolls a sequence down into a single value by repeatedly applying a function to pairs of items
not built in by default, needs importing from functools
example: from functools import reduce
nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]
total = reduce(lambda a, b: a + b, nums)
result 10
works through the list like this: ((1+2)+3)+4

## zip

pairs up items from two or more sequences, position by position
example: names = ["Ben", "Sam"]
ages = [20, 22]
list(zip(names, ages))
result [('Ben', 20), ('Sam', 22)]
stops as soon as the shortest sequence runs out, even if the others still have items left

## enumerate

loops over a sequence while also keeping track of the index of each item, without manually managing a counter
example: fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]
for index, fruit in enumerate(fruits):
print(index, fruit) result
0 apple
1 banana
2 cherry

## any

checks a sequence and returns True if at least one item is truthy, False if every item is falsy
example: nums = [0, 0, 3, 0]
any(nums)
result True nums = [0, 0, 0]
any(nums)
result False

## all

checks a sequence and returns True only if every single item is truthy, False if even one isn't
example: nums = [1, 2, 3]
all(nums)
result True nums = [1, 0, 3]
all(nums)
result False
any and all are especially handy combined with a generator expression, checking a condition across a whole list without writing a manual loop: all(x > 0 for x in [1, 2, 3])
result True